    "first_seen": "2026-11-06"
   },
   {
    "quote": "Most \"well-sourced\" claims are one source wearing costumes: a stat born in a vendor's report, echoed by a blog, cited by a journalist, and now \"widely reported\" — three citations, one origin, zero verification. Triangulation is the discipline of asking two questions before repeating anything:",
    "url": "https://skillmd.com/skills/mohitagw15856/source-triangulation",
    "pass": 3,
    "first_seen": "2026-11-06"
   },
   {
    "quote": "Framework: The Triangulation Rules",
    "url": "https://skillmd.com/skills/mohitagw15856/source-triangulation",
    "pass": 3,
    "first_seen": "2026-11-06"
   },
   {
    "quote": "established → state it with the cite · single-sourced → attribute inline (\"a 2024 Gartner survey found…\") · contested → the range with both cites · unverifiable → don't repeat it, or repeat it explicitly as unsourced folklore if rhetorically necessary. The phrasing rule is what makes triangulation",
    "url": "https://skillmd.com/skills/mohitagw15856/source-triangulation",
    "pass": 3,
    "first_seen": "2026-11-06"
   },
   {
    "quote": "Triangulation: \"[the claim]\"",
    "url": "https://skillmd.com/skills/mohitagw15856/source-triangulation",
